<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · China, Tibet and Mongolia</p><h1 class="st-title">Eastern Liao</h1><p class="st-meta">1213 CE – 1269 CE · capital Xianping</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Khitan kingdom in Manchuria founded by Yelü Liuge in revolt against the Jurchen, which survived as a Mongol vassal until 1269.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>In 1212, as Mongol attacks weakened the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/jin-dynasty-1115-1234">Jin dynasty</a> (1115-1234), the Khitan officer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yelu-liuge">Yelü Liuge</a> rebelled in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liaodong">Liaodong</a> and allied himself with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genghis-khan">Genghis Khan</a>. In 1213 he proclaimed himself king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liao-dynasty">Liao</a>, reviving the name of the fallen <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/liao-dynasty">Liao dynasty</a>, and defeated Jin armies sent against him.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The kingdom drew on Khitan soldiers and settlers in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manchuria">Manchuria</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yelu-liuge">Yelü Liuge</a> chose loyalty to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> over full independence, submitting in person to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genghis-khan">Genghis Khan</a> in 1215. Some of his followers rejected this, set up a rival regime, and fled into <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/goryeo">Goryeo</a>, where Mongol and Korean forces destroyed them in 1219.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yelu-liuge">Yelü Liuge's</a> death in 1220 his widow and son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yelu-xuedu">Yelü Xuedu</a> governed as hereditary <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassals</a> of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/mongol-empire">Mongol Empire</a>. The kingdom was gradually reduced and was abolished in 1269 under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kublai-khan">Kublai</a>, its lands absorbed into Yuan administration.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="kingdom, 1213 CE – 1269 CE"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M22 72 V40 L36 54 L50 28 L64 54 L78 40 V72 Z"/><path d="M22 80 H78"/><circle cx="50" cy="24" r="3"/><circle cx="22" cy="36" r="2.5"/><circle cx="78" cy="36" r="2.5"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">kingdom</p><p class="st-emblem-region">China, Tibet and Mongolia</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:83.84%;width:1.20%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1213 CE – 1269 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Xianping</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Yelü Liuge, Yelü Xuedu</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Buddhism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Khitan, Middle Chinese</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/china">China</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Eastern Liao (1213-1269)</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1241&amp;focus=eastern-liao" aria-label="Eastern Liao on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7684 665 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Eastern Liao lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M8004 921L8012 829M8004 921L7559 780"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M8012 829h0M7559 780h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M8004 921h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M8004 921h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its capital. <a href="/atlas?year=1241&amp;focus=eastern-liao">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
